# =========================================================================
# Where wear COMES FROM.
#
# A worn node is `__worn__` on top of `__undamaged__` - it still works, just not as
# well, and it wants a maintenance work order to be itself again. Nothing here
# invents that work: it arrives from what the crew actually did.
#
# Three sources, in the order they matter to a player:
#
#   FIRING     a beam hit wears the beam pool; a launch wears the tubes.
#   TAKING IT  a hit wears the shield facing that ate it.
#   TIME       a per-minute beat ages every node, plus drive wear from throttle.
#
# The rates all live in the library beside the thresholds
# (`WEAR_PER_BEAM_HIT` and friends) so `grid_set_wear_tuning(beam_hit=...)` moves
# them at runtime. `grid_set_wear_tuning(upkeep_rate=0)` turns the time source off
# entirely for a mission that only wants combat to cost something.
#
# A NEW FILE on purpose: a top-level label appended to an existing .mast falls
# through from whatever preceded it, silently.
# =========================================================================


# --- firing: a beam hit wears the beam that fired it ---------------------
# DAMAGE_ORIGIN_ID is what DEALT the damage. Measured raw in engine 1.3.5 (see the
# note in cosmos_dev/mock/sbs.py): a BEAM hit carries origin = the shooter with
# parent 0, while a TORPEDO hit carries origin = the TORPEDO and parent = the
# launching ship. So origin is a ship only on the beam branch - which is the only
# branch that reads it. Torpedo wear comes from the launch route below instead,
# where the shooter is unambiguous.
//damage/object if EVENT.sub_tag == "beam"
    ->END if not has_role(DAMAGE_ORIGIN_ID, "__player__")
    grid_wear_system(DAMAGE_ORIGIN_ID, "beam", WEAR_PER_BEAM_HIT)
    ->END


# --- firing: a launch wears the tubes ------------------------------------
# Launching is the tube's own work, so this is charged on the shot rather than on
# the hit - a torpedo that misses wore the tube just the same.
//launch/missile
    ->END if not has_role(LAUNCH_ORIGIN_ID, "__player__")
    grid_wear_system(LAUNCH_ORIGIN_ID, "torpedo", WEAR_PER_TUBE_SHOT)
    ->END


# --- taking it: a hit wears the shield facing that ate it ----------------
# The same call damage/destroy.mast makes to find the struck facing, with the same
# two guards: the engine can deliver a damage event with no source point (a mine, a
# scripted hit), and the target can already be gone.
//damage/object if has_role(DAMAGE_TARGET_ID, "__player__")
    ->END if EVENT.source_point is None
    hit_ship = to_object(DAMAGE_TARGET_ID)
    ->END if hit_ship is None
    wear_face = sim.get_shield_hit_index_source(EVENT.source_point, to_engine_object(DAMAGE_TARGET_ID))
    grid_wear_shield_hit(DAMAGE_TARGET_ID, wear_face)
    ->END


# --- time and travel: one beat per ship ----------------------------------
# Started when the interior exists, because that is when there are nodes to wear.
# set_interval is the right driver here rather than a Python tick callback: it stops
# on its own when the ship is deleted, a paused sim does not advance it, and it
# cannot take the sim down - a raising TickDispatcher body puts the engine on an
# ErrorPage whose Resume re-raises.
//shared/signal/grid_interior_built
    ->END if not has_role(SHIP_ID, "__player__")
    set_interval(SHIP_ID, "wear_upkeep", "wear_upkeep_beat", seconds=60)
    ->END


# //shared/signal, not //signal: this SPAWNS wear. A plain //signal route runs once
# per connected console, so a five-console bridge would age its ship five times a
# minute and a solo player once. SIGNAL_ROUTING.md.
//shared/signal/wear_upkeep_beat
    wear_ship = to_object(TIMER_AGENT_ID)
    ->END if wear_ship is None
    # The interval stops itself when the agent is deleted, but an exploded ship is
    # still an agent - and its grid is being torn down.
    if has_role(TIMER_AGENT_ID, "exploded"):
        clear_interval(TIMER_AGENT_ID, "wear_upkeep")
        ->END

    # TIME: every working node ages a little.
    grid_wear_upkeep(TIMER_AGENT_ID)
    # TRAVEL: impulse or warp, whichever the ship is actually using. The throttle
    # read and the warp split live in the library so they are unit-testable - the
    # engine answers None for a blob nothing has set, and a failing expression stops
    # the command, so an unguarded compare here would silently end this route.
    grid_wear_travel(TIMER_AGENT_ID)
    ->END
